    "growth_rate_source": "normalized_ffo_per_share_3yr_cagr (FY2022 $2.99 -> FY2025 $3.48 on diluted shares, old post-SBC definition both ends)",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR (7.2% 3yr) and revenue CAGR (12.2% 3yr) are driven by share issuance (shares 403m -> 463m FY2022-FY2025, 529m by Sep-2026), not per-share compounding; GAAP EPS is depreciation-dominated and swings through zero, so no EPS CAGR is given. Normalized FFO per share (5.2% 3yr; 4.7% over the four years from FY2021) is the meaningful series. FFO history in the CSV starts FY2021, so no 5-year figure.",
    "notes": "fcf_* here is Normalized FFO per diluted share, the closest disclosed proxy for owner cash flow. Recent pace is faster: 9M-2026 Normalized FFO per diluted share $2.90 vs $2.65 restated 9M-2025, +9.4%."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "AFFO per existing share grows 10% next year and fades to 3.5% by year 9. The driver is SHOP, now roughly 59% of NOI, where same-store NOI has grown 15-16% for eight straight quarters on occupancy gains (same-store 87.0% -> 92.1% in two years) and RevPOR up ~7%. Occupancy cannot compound much beyond the low 90s, so the path steps down each year toward rent-growth-only. OM&R contributes 2-3%; NNN is flat after the Q3-2026 same-store decline of 5.2%. Capitalised at an 8.0% cost of equity with 3.0% terminal growth.",
    "bull": "The demographic tailwind and a decade of under-building keep SHOP NOI compounding at double digits for three more years, with rate rather than occupancy doing the work, and the cost of equity settles at 7.5% as the market keeps paying a premium for the growth.",
    "bear": "SHOP growth halves as occupancy tops out and wage inflation returns; NNN lease resets continue to cut rent; maintenance capex takes a larger share of NOI. The market re-rates healthcare REITs to a 9% cost of equity."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Ventas is valued on the cash an owner could actually take out: Normalized FFO less stock compensation, maintenance capex and straight-line rent, capitalised at the cost of equity. Debt is not deducted because the flow is already after interest. Only organic, same-store growth is credited to the existing share; acquisitions funded by new equity are assumed to earn their cost of capital.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "AFFO is derived, not disclosed: all 'other capital expenditures' (~$450m/yr) are treated as maintenance. If true FAD capex is nearer $350m, AFFO per share is ~6% higher and value rises proportionately.",
      "External growth is excluded. Ventas issued 47m shares in nine months at ~$87 to fund acquisitions; if those are accretive per share (issuing at an implied ~4.5% FFO yield to buy at 7%+ yields), the model understates growth.",
      "Terminal value is most of the result and moves ~15% for each 0.5pt of cost of equity; a healthcare REIT trading at ~31x AFFO is a long-duration asset.",
      "SHOP is an operating business with labour-cost and occupancy risk, unlike triple-net rent; the 2020-2022 experience (Normalized FFO/share ~$2.90-2.99) shows the downside.",
      "The 10-year Treasury yield behind the cost of equity is an assumption (~4.3%), not a fetched quote.",
      "$12.4bn of debt at fixed rates below current market reprices upward as it matures, a drag on FFO growth not separately modelled."
    ]
  },
